gical site in Jämtland, Sweden, featuring rock carvings (petroglyphs) created approximately 7,000 years ago during the Mesolithic period [1]. The carvings are located on large boulders along the Gärdesån river in the village of Gärde, depicting primarily moose (elk) representations [1]. These petroglyphs are among the oldest known rock art in Sweden and provide rare visual evidence of Stone Age hunting societies in northern Scandinavia [1]. The site is situated in Krokom Municipality, approximately 70 kilometers northwest of Östersund, in a traditional agricultural area with a name dating back to 1553 [2].'' -> ''Gärde is a prehistoric archaeological site in Jämtland, Sweden, featuring rock carvings (petroglyphs) created approximately 7,000 years ago [1] [2]. The carvings are located along the Gärdesån river in the village of Gärde, depicting primarily moose (elk) representations [1] [2] [3]. The site is situated in Krokom Municipality, approximately 70 kilometers northwest of Östersund, in a traditional agricultural area with a name dating back to 1553 [1] [3].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385727", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385728", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-10000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:413995", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:413996", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''-4501''"}]'::jsonb),
