d by opus caementicium cores. It was one of three aqueducts serving Merida. The structure is part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Merida, a UNESCO World Heritage Site.'' -> ''The Acueducto de los Milagros (\"Aqueduct of Miracles\") is a Roman aqueduct built to carry water from the Proserpina reservoir to the colony of Augusta Emerita (modern Merida, Spain) [1] [2] [3]. Its remaining stretch of 830 meters features pillars standing 25 meters high, constructed with alternating courses of granite ashlar and brick bonded by opus caementicium cores [4] [5]. It was one of three aqueducts serving Merida [3] [6]. The structure is part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Merida, a UNESCO World Heritage Site [3] [7].''"}]'::jsonb),
