ote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4311:chunk-0001: description \"Su Kemeri is a Roman aqueduct at the ancient city of Phaselis on Turkey''s southern coast near Antalya. The aqueduct stretches approximately 450 m, beginning at about 13 m above sea level with a gradient of 0.22%, carrying water between the sea and a swamp toward the agora. Phaselis was founded by Rhodian colonists in the 7th century BC and became one of the most important trading ports along the Lycian coast. The aqueduct''s well-preserved piers and arches remain one of the site''s most prominent features along the northern harbor.\" -> \"Su Kemeri is a Roman aqueduct at the ancient city of Phaselis on Turkey''s southern coast near Antalya [1] [2] [3]. Phaselis was founded by Rhodian colonists in the 7th century BC and became one of the most important trading ports along the Lycian coast [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349032", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s004: source_url ''https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q135582591'' -> ''https://zenodo.org/records/14577067''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385776", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385777", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:414007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:414008", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''1''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('59af6742-7a6d-45ba-9cb7-9fd0de0c51c9'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:59af6742-7a6d-45ba-9cb7-9fd0de0c51c9:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''8'' restored on Casma-Sechin Culture', '95f1322d835fcbfef34e751ca32056f3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Archaeological site'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43531", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0075:chunk-0001: description ''The Casma-Sechin culture (c. 3600-200 BC) encompasses a large concentration of prehistoric ruins in the Casma and Sechin river valleys of coastal Peru. Sechin Bajo, with a circular plaza radiocarbon-dated to 3500 BC, may contain the oldest monumental architecture in the Americas. Sechin Alto, a flat-topped pyramid measuring 300x250m and 35m high, was the largest construction in the Americas when built around 1600-1400 BC. Cerro Sechin, discovered in 1937 by Julio C. Tello, features megalithic walls with bas-relief carvings graphically depicting human sacrifice. The size and uniformity of these monuments suggest Sechin Alto served as the administrative center of a polity uniting the valleys under one government.'' -> ''The Casma–Sechin culture (c. 3600 BCE–200 BCE) of Peru refers to the large concentration of pre-historic ruins in the valleys of the Casma River and its tributary the Sechin River and along the nearby coast of the Pacific Ocean [1]. The ruins include major archaeological sites such as Sechin Bajo, Sechin Alto, Cerro Sechin, Mojeque, Chankillo, and Taukachi-Konkan, as well as other smaller sites [1]. Most of these inland sites are found in the river valleys about 20 kilometres distant from the ocean [1]. The seaside sites of Huaynuná and Las Haldas are found about 20 kilometres north and south of the mouth of the Casma River on the coast [1]. The area of the Casma/Sechin culture extended about 40 kilometres inland from the sea [1]. By 3000 BC, at a few locations in coastal Peru, including the Casma/Sechin valleys, a sizable population was present and undertaking large projects such as building pyramids and other ceremonial and monumental structures [1]. Radiocarbon dating indicates a plaza at Sechin Bajo was constructed in 3500 BCE [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:117647", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s004: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150314", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/59af6742/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/59af6742/Frontis_Templo_Sechin.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311235", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/59af6742/Frontis_Templo_Sechin.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/59af6742/Sechín_Archaeological_site_-_relief_(head_profile_left).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
