    ('5aaf8925-6b79-4b61-9933-be252c6f2ff6'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:5aaf8925-6b79-4b61-9933-be252c6f2ff6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''6'' restored on Tomb of the Eagles', '3a032a2d92588ba9b8acc4ce4cce2a6b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 27 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2094:chunk-0001: description ''The Tomb of the Eagles (Isbister Chambered Cairn) is a Neolithic chambered tomb on the cliffs of South Ronaldsay in Orkney, Scotland, built around 3000 BC. Discovered by farmer Ronald Simison in 1958, the 8.2 m long rectangular chamber is divided into five compartments by pairs of stone orthostats. The tomb contained roughly 16,000 human bones from about 340 individuals and 70 talons of the white-tailed sea eagle, though the eagle bones were deposited up to 1,000 years after the cairn ceased its original use. Entry requires crawling through a 3 m tunnel.'' -> ''The Tomb of the Eagles, or Isbister Chambered Cairn, is a Neolithic chambered tomb located on a cliff edge at Isbister on South Ronaldsay in Orkney, Scotland [1]. The site was discovered by Ronald Simison when digging flagstones in 1958; he conducted a limited excavation and removed some bones and skulls at that time but filled in the site with dirt [1]. A more extensive excavation was started in 1976, and \"an enormous amount of material was removed\", according to a report published in 2002 [1]. At the site, 16,000 human bones have been found, from at least 324 individuals, but no intact skeletons [1]. Mixed with the human bones were talons and bones from eight to 20 birds [1]. Some believed that the eagle was a totem animal for the individuals entombed here, but subsequent research indicated that the bird artifacts were added at a later time [1]. These were identified as predominantly belonging to the white-tailed sea eagle (Haliaeetus albicilla) [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150341", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-012: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Isbister_Cairn_Tomb_of_the_Eagles_04.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5aaf8925/Isbister Chambered Cairn 20110524.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:311256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5aaf8925/Isbister Chambered Cairn 20110524.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5aaf8925/Isbister_Cairn_Tomb_of_the_Eagles_04.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
