    ('68181f11-04ab-4bfb-aedb-0c7bc0ebb2f8'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:68181f11-04ab-4bfb-aedb-0c7bc0ebb2f8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''6'' restored on Listoghil', '6028f721bc4a5df796131f613e2004c0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 27 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34837", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0034:chunk-0001: description \"Listoghil is the central monument of the Carrowmore megalithic cemetery in County Sligo, Ireland, constructed around 3500 BC. Designated Carrowmore 51, it is 34 metres in diameter and the only cairn in the complex, with surrounding satellite tombs oriented toward it. The chamber contained both cremated and unburned human remains, and the covering stone bears marks that may be the sole example of megalithic art at Carrowmore. Carrowmore is one of Ireland''s four great megalithic cemetery complexes and represents some of the earliest passage tombs in Ireland.\" -> \"Listoghil (Irish: Lios an tSeagail) is the large central monument in the Carrowmore group of prehistoric tombs in County Sligo, Ireland [1]. The Carrowmore group consists of about 35 monuments surrounding Listoghil [1]. It was numbered as Carrowmore 51 by George Petrie in 1837 and this designation is still used [1]. Listoghil stands 59 meters above sea level at the geographic centre of the Cúil Iorra Peninsula, c. 3 km from Sligo town [1]. Listoghil is larger than its satellites, being about 34 meters in diameter; the satellites average about 15 meters [1]. By the end of the 19th century the tomb had been investigated by antiquarians of the period who recorded finding ''bones of horses'', charred wood and a worked flint javelin head [1]. In the late 1990s, Goran Burenhult, a Swedish archaeologist, partly excavated Listoghil [1]. The chamber at the heart of the mound is a dolmen-like structure, with 6 orthostats [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151761", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/County Sligo - Carrowmore Passage Tomb - 20180708152404.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312117", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/County Sligo - Carrowmore Passage Tomb - 20180708152404.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/Central_Chamber.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
