d the flat stone platform was the bema, the \"stepping stone\" or speakers\\'' platform [1]. In 1910, excavations began at the site by the Greek Archaeological Society and confirmed the site as the Pnyx [1]. Large-scale excavations were conducted at times between 1930 and 1937 by Homer Thompson [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118950", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Megalithic stones'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152420", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/708e01e6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/708e01e6/Ancient ruins on the Pnyx on September 19, 2020.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312262", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/708e01e6/Ancient ruins on the Pnyx on September 19, 2020.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/708e01e6/Pnyx_Bema_2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
