ed''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:414011", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:414012", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07d_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''1000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7ceced42-5339-4dc0-84da-5c11f6898879'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:7ceced42-5339-4dc0-84da-5c11f6898879:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''6'' restored on Gavrinis', 'c2d177f663231aa25a7b418111550211', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 27 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2117:chunk-0001: description \"Gavrinis is a small island in the Gulf of Morbihan, Brittany, France, home to a Neolithic passage tomb built c. 4200–4000 BC, making it one of the world''s oldest surviving buildings[1]. The stones lining its passage and chamber are covered in megalithic art[1]. The tomb is part of a broader complex of Breton megalithic monuments including Carnac and Locmariaquer[1]. It is closely comparable to other major passage tombs such as Barnenez in Brittany and Newgrange in Ireland[1].\" -> ''Gavrinis is a small island in the Gulf of Morbihan in Brittany, France [1]. The island is famous for its important passage grave, a megalithic monument from the Neolithic period, belonging to the same broad context as the Breton megaliths of Carnac and Locmariaquer [1]. The rich internal decorations make Gavrinis one of the major treasuries of European megalithic art [1]. The tomb is also remarkable for the care taken in its construction and its good preservation [1]. The first excavations took place in 1835, when the internal chamber was discovered [1]. The chamber is reached from outside by a 14 m-long corridor or passage [1]. Of the 29 orthostat slabs that form the sides of the passage, 23 are decorated with carved symbols and patterns [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120179", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Cairn''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:120180", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s006: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gavrinis'' -> ''https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cairn_de_Gavrinis''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153875", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7ceced42/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7ceced42/Cairn de Gavrinis par drone - vue 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313080", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7ceced42/Cairn de Gavrinis par drone - vue 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7ceced42/Cairn_Gavrinis_entrance.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
