 [1]. As with many other Inca sites, it also has an irrigation system [1]. The purpose of these depressions is uncertain, but their depth, design, and orientation with respect to wind and sun creates a temperature difference of as much as 15 °C between the top and the bottom [1]. The wide temperature differences in the terraces have created microclimates, similar to what is achieved in greenhouses in modern times [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Moray_-_Qechuyoq.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray, 2023 (13).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:332705", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray, 2023 (13).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray - Qechuyoq.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9755c087-8b24-46e2-9785-b5ac6c91f3ea'::uuid, 'total_power', '23', '24', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:9755c087-8b24-46e2-9785-b5ac6c91f3ea:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: total_power ''24'' restored on Moray', 'd9ab50f617ee3dd3df3c26912f9cfe9f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 23 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 1 + 7 + 10 + 4 + 1"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29650", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0319:chunk-0001: site_type ''Geological interest'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44833", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0100:chunk-0001: description ''Moray is an Inca archaeological site on a high plateau at 3,500 m elevation, northwest of Cusco, Peru, featuring concentric circular terraces descending up to 150 m deep. Believed to have functioned as an agricultural research laboratory, each terrace created its own microclimate with temperature differences of up to 15 degrees C between top and bottom. The Inca imported soils from diverse regions and tested over 250 plant species including potatoes, quinoa, and maize. Discovered from the air in 1932, the site may date in part to the earlier Wari culture (6th-10th c.).'' -> ''Moray (Quechua: Muray) is an archaeological site in Peru approximately 50 kilometres northwest of Cuzco on a high plateau at about 3,500 metres and just west of the village of Maras [1]. The site contains Inca ruins, mostly consisting of several terraced circular depressions, the largest of which is approximately 30 m deep [1]. As with many other Inca sites, it also has an irrigation system [1]. The purpose of these depressions is uncertain, but their depth, design, and orientation with respect to wind and sun creates a temperature difference of as much as 15 °C between the top and the bottom [1]. The wide temperature differences in the terraces have created microclimates, similar to what is achieved in greenhouses in modern times [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Moray_-_Qechuyoq.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray, 2023 (13).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:332705", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray, 2023 (13).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9755c087/Moray - Qechuyoq.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('990741ec-f247-483a-8b8e-29f634e63f96'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:990741ec-f247-483a-8b8e-29f634e63f96:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''7'' restored on Deva Victrix', '7be7d2fcf8f6f9a4471eb735ed20a726', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Fortification'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86238", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2136:chunk-0001: description ''Deva Victrix was a legionary fortress and settlement in the Roman province of Britannia, located on the site of modern Chester, England [1]. The fortress was originally built by Legio II Adiutrix during the 70s AD as Roman forces advanced north against the Brigantes [1][2]. It was later rebuilt and expanded by Legio XX Valeria Victrix, which gave the fortress its lasting name [1]. A major reconstruction took place in the early 3rd century [1]. The legion remained stationed at Deva until the late 4th or early 5th century, when the fortress fell into disuse as Roman authority in Britain declined [1][3].'' -> \"Deva Victrix, or simply Deva, was a legionary fortress and town in the Roman province of Britannia on the site of the modern city of Chester [1]. The fortress was built by the Legio II Adiutrix in the 70s AD as the Roman army advanced north against the Brigantes, and rebuilt completely over the next few decades by the Legio XX Valeria Victrix [1]. In the early 3rd century the fortress was again rebuilt [1]. A civilian settlement, or canaba, grew around the fortress [1]. Chester''s Roman Amphitheatre, south-east of the fortress, is the largest-known military amphitheatre in Britain [1]. The civilian settlement remained after the Romans departed, eventually becoming the present-day city of Chester [1]. The fortress contained barracks, granaries, headquarters, military baths, and an unusual elliptical building which had it been finished may have been intended to act as the governor of Britain''s headquarters [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156716", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/990741ec/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/990741ec/Camp de légion de Deva Victrix.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314734", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/990741ec/Camp de légion de Deva Victrix.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/990741ec/ChesterCan4.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
