    ('bf30fac5-d015-46c4-b340-74e5614ce274'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:bf30fac5-d015-46c4-b340-74e5614ce274:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''7'' restored on El Azuzul', '7cfdf8ce580a69118572100ceb1199e8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Archaeological site'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87172", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2163:chunk-0001: description ''El Azuzul is an Olmec archaeological site in Veracruz, Mexico, located a few kilometers south of the San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán complex and generally considered contemporary with it [1]. Named for the ranch on which it is located, the site is part of the Loma del Zapote complex and occupies higher elevations north of the confluence of two ancient river courses within the Coatzacoalcos River system [1]. The site lies upstream of the monumental earthworks at Potrero Nuevo, which is part of the San Lorenzo complex [1].'' -> ''El Azuzul is an Olmec archaeological site in Veracruz, Mexico, a few kilometers south of the San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán complex and generally considered contemporary with it (perhaps 1100 to 800 BCE) [1]. Named for the ranch on which it is located, El Azuzul is part of the Loma del Zapote complex [1]. The site occupies the higher elevations north of the confluence of two ancient river courses, a part of the Coatzacoalcos River system [1]. It is upstream of the monumental earthworks at Potrero Nuevo, which is part of the San Lorenzo complex [1]. El Azuzul is best known for two pairs of monumental sculptures, now on exhibit at the Xalapa Museum of Anthropology, Xalapa [1]. These statues were found on the south side of the large pyramid/hill on the site, intact and apparently undisturbed since they were placed there in Pre-Classic times [1]. The first pair of statues, described as \"some of the greatest masterpieces of Olmec art\", are nearly identical seated human figures [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:107001", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s017: site_type ''Pyramid complex'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242702", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: site_type None -> ''Archaeological site''"}]'::jsonb),
