l hearths, and precious objects left on the hill, presumably as offerings [1]. Probably around 3000 BC a Class II henge was constructed with the hilltop being surrounded by a bank outside a ditch about 12 ft wide cut over 3 ft into the rock [1]. Some time later a Bronze Age ritual added a small stone and clay cairn just off-centre inside the monument [1]. Under the cairn traces were found of at least one burial, with wooden objects (perhaps a mask and club) and beaker people style pottery which indicates a date around 2000 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161479", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c59b8485/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c59b8485/Cairnpapple Hill alt1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c59b8485-583b-4c64-8d5b-61e046ed3437'::uuid, 'total_power', '25', '26', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:c59b8485-583b-4c64-8d5b-61e046ed3437:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: total_power ''26'' restored on Cairnpapple Hill', 'a12f329b4fb49208721dae0909ceb0df', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 25 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 8 + 2 + 9 + 5 + 1"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37495", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0057:chunk-0001: description \"Cairnpapple Hill is one of mainland Scotland''s most important prehistoric sites, used as a ritual center for over 4,000 years from the 4th millennium BC to the early Christian era. The earliest traces are six hearths dating from c. 3500 BC, containing pottery and stone-axe fragments from Wales and Cumbria. These were succeeded by a Neolithic henge monument featuring a bank 60m across with a ring of 24 timber posts. During the Bronze Age the site transitioned to funerary use, with cist burials accompanied by Beaker pottery and a wooden face mask, later covered by successive cairns. Excavated by Professor Piggott in 1947-48.\" -> ''Cairnpapple Hill is a hill with a dominating position in central lowland Scotland with views from coast to coast [1]. It was used and re-used as a major ritual site for around 4,000 years [1]. In the 19th century the site was completely concealed by trees, then in 1947–1948 excavations by Stuart Piggott found a series of ritual monuments from successive prehistoric periods [1]. Neolithic rituals began around 3500 BC with signs of small hearths, and precious objects left on the hill, presumably as offerings [1]. Probably around 3000 BC a Class II henge was constructed with the hilltop being surrounded by a bank outside a ditch about 12 ft wide cut over 3 ft into the rock [1]. Some time later a Bronze Age ritual added a small stone and clay cairn just off-centre inside the monument [1]. Under the cairn traces were found of at least one burial, with wooden objects (perhaps a mask and club) and beaker people style pottery which indicates a date around 2000 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161479", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c59b8485/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c59b8485/Cairnpapple Hill alt1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5b89e54-5127-4500-b6b4-e63f35efdc8d'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:c5b89e54-5127-4500-b6b4-e63f35efdc8d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''8'' restored on Machu Pirqa', '39fd04adc63df0e39b13d13b123dbc97', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (None, ''1000 - 1500 AD'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81284", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2027:chunk-0001: description ''Machu Pirqa (Quechua for \"old wall\") is a Chachapoyas funerary site in Peru\\''s Amazonas Region, situated at 1,900 m on the left bank of the Kuntichaka River near the entrance of Shihual Cave. Dating to approximately 1100-1300 AD, its stone buildings were constructed with mud cement to house the remains of Chachapoya dignitaries. The structures feature characteristic high-relief friezes with rhomboidal and zigzag patterns. The site lies along the Qhapaq Nan trail network between Chachapoyas and Kuelap.'' -> ''Machu Pirqa is an archaeological site of the Chachapoyas culture in Peru [1]. It is located in the Amazonas Region, Chachapoyas Province, Magdalena District [1]. Machu Pirqa is situated at a height of about 1,900 metres on the left bank of the river Kuntichaka, an affluent of the Utcubamba River [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108008", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d