d [1]. The site was first excavated in 1916 by whaling Captain George Comer, ice master of the Crocker Land Expedition''s relief team, and of members of Knud Rasmussen''s Second Danish Thule Expedition who were in the area charting the North Greenland coast [1]. The site was excavated by Erik Holtved in 1935 to 1937, and again in 1946 to 1947 [1]. The site shows signs of having been inhabited from the 14th to the 20th century although Holtved reports that the 17th and 18th centuries are poorly represented [1]. The site contains about 26 house ruins and several middens distributed over an area of about 120 metres in width and stretching over 400 metres inland with the midden which Comer excavated located at its south end [1]. The vast majority of harpoon heads found are of the open socket type typical of the Thule culture [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108835", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''1000 - 1500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108836", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108837", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162876", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cecf3b1e/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243679", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243680", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''1301''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243681", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: site_type None -> ''Archaeological site''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d0e007ba-24cd-4929-b135-0b907397e531'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:d0e007ba-24cd-4929-b135-0b907397e531:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''6'' restored on Long Low, Wetton', '21147261fbe2a86d11d1ef36e55e6e59', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Cairn'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 27 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42124", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0060:chunk-0001: description ''Long Low is a Neolithic and Bronze Age site about 2 km southeast of Wetton in Staffordshire, consisting of two round cairns linked by a connecting bank -- a layout unique in England. The northern cairn is 23 metres in diameter and 2.4 metres high. Excavated between 1846 and 1851, the primary chamber yielded remains of at least 13 individuals along with three finely chipped flint arrowheads and animal bones. The connecting ridge contained scattered human bone fragments and charcoal, suggesting prolonged funerary use spanning the late Neolithic to Bronze Age.'' -> ''Long Low is a Neolithic and Bronze Age site in the English county of Staffordshire [1]. It is about 2 km SE of Wetton [1]. It consists of two round cairns linked by a connecting bank – an unusual layout and one that is unique in England [1]. The northern cairn measures 23 metres in diameter and survives to a height of 2.4 metres [1]. It appears to be related to other chamber tombs of the Peak District group and was excavated by Samuel Carrington in 1849 [1]. Carrington found a burial chamber built from limestone orthostats with a paved floor [1]. The bones of thirteen individuals were recovered as well as three leaf-shaped flint arrowheads [1]. The smaller southern cairn is 15 metres across and survives to 1.2 metres in height following extensive modern damage [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108856", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108857", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253623", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253624", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387400", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387401", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401859", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401860", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_start None -> ''-4500''"}]'::jsonb),
