]. It was also known as the Alphitopolis Stoa, from the Greek word alphita (barley flour) [1]. The Stoa was a long building which was used for the storage of imported cereals, mainly barley and wheat [1]. The cereals were then transported to the main city of Athens for consumption [1]. The Stoa also supplied the Athenian fleet of triremes and, during times of shortage, supplied the people directly [1]. The Stoa had an open facade and colonnade on one side and on the other side had shops made up of rectangular rooms [1]. The rooms alternated between small and large sizes and each shop had a space for supplying cereals and a grain storage area [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125550", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125551", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125552", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163716", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d85b55ee/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:253702", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s019: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
