    ('f28a2d38-f5a9-49f2-83c8-53bb4c61cae4'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07d-rollback:f28a2d38-f5a9-49f2-83c8-53bb4c61cae4:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07d: mystery ''7'' restored on Fectio', '57ba581a22a142913876e303795c3bd4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Fortification'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export ddb8b67997033964 (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 118 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07c''s own export (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88396", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2199:chunk-0001: description \"Fectio was a Roman castellum (fortress) in the province Germania Inferior, established in 4 or 5 AD at the strategic confluence where the river Vecht branched from the Rhine, leading to Lake Flevo (later the Zuiderzee). Located near modern Vechten in Bunnik, Utrecht, it served as a key military installation along the Roman northern frontier, controlling river traffic and defending the empire''s boundary.\" -> ''Fectio was a Roman castellum in the province Germania Inferior established in the year 4 or 5 AD [1]. It was located at the place where the river Vecht (Fectio) branched off from the Rhine, leading to Lake Flevo, which was later to become the Zuiderzee [1]. This was near the modern hamlet of Vechten in the municipality Bunnik, Utrecht [1]. Imperial Roman coinage reveals that the castellum was built by order of Tiberius, then engaged in his campaign of 4–5 AD [1]. In 40 AD the emperor Caligula visited Fectio during the trip to Lugdunum Batavorum, the ancient Brittenburg [1]. Under the emperor Claudius, Fectio became part of the Rhine limes [1]. Then around 70 AD, at the time of the Batava revolt, the castellum was completely burned [1]. The archeological site contains the remains of a fort, port, cemetery, and a civilian settlement [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165973", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-033: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f28a2d38/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f28a2d38/De countouren van het hoofdgebouw van Castellum Fectio in Bunnk.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
